    template: 'microCredential',
    description: 'Partnering with people to live well, every day. Registered nurses supporting people with diabetes, from children to older adults.',
    text: 'Diabetes Nursing N8020 Partnering with people to live well, every day. Registered nurses supporting people with diabetes, from children to older adults. Endocrine and diabetes services Primary care Aged and disability care Diabetes nurses are central to good patient care and to helping people manage their own condition. This specialty offers a systematic introduction to the fundamentals of diabetes nursing across healthcare settings. You will examine the causes, risk factors, pathophysiology, symptoms, therapies, interprofessional care, psychosocial support and nursing care of people with type 1 and type 2 diabetes, and their families and carers, through a person-centred lens. Topics: Causes, risk factors and pathophysiology of type 1 and type 2 diabetes Therapies and interprofessional care Psychosocial support for people, families and carers Self-care management and education Diabetes care across acute, chronic, aged care, disability and rehabilitation settings Parent award: Graduate Certificate in Advanced Nursing. Credential type: Proposed non-award micro-credential. Duration: 7 weeks (orientation week + 6 class weeks). Delivery: Fully online, 12 credit points at AQF Level 8. Fees: $3,000 per unit ($250 per credit point). Next intake: 8 March 2027, then July and November. Who can enrol: Domestic students and eligible non-student visa holders (proposed) Credit and pathway: N8020 Diabetes Nursing, N8021 Professional Reflection in Diabetes Nursing, N9001, Graduate Certificate in Advanced Nursing.',
