    text: 'Industry Hub Partnership Specialist capability, grown where your patients are. A proposed way for hospitals, aged care providers and health services to upskill nurses one specialty at a time, with IHM as an academic partner. Start a partner conversation (opens official IHM site in a new tab) IHM Industry Hub (opens official IHM site in a new tab) The proposition What a partnership could include All arrangements below are drawn from IHM’s draft proposal and are subject to approval. Partner nomination Nominate staff into a specialty aligned with your workforce plan. IHM admits each learner individually against the published eligibility rules. Roster-aware cohorts Closed cohorts timetabled around rosters, with a named IHM academic liaison. Minimum cohort size is to be confirmed. Partner rate and invoicing Options proposed include a partner rate, a single invoice to your organisation, or employee-paid with reimbursement. Rates are to be confirmed. Progress reporting A partner progress report each study period covering enrolment, progression and completion, respecting student privacy. Co-design and endorsement Partners may co-design future micro-credentials or endorse existing ones, with endorsement stated on the relevant page. Pathway to the award Completers can be supported toward the parent award, with credit assessed under IHM policy, strengthening succession and retention. Commercial terms: Partner rate Minimum cohort size Invoicing terms You will improve your skills and knowledge in the area you work in; your patients will get better, safer, more compassionate care; and your organisation will see the benefit on the ward and in the community. What partners can say to their staff, in plain language. Adapted from the IHM proposal. Workforce alignment Map specialties to your service needs Twelve specialties across four care areas, each based on a named GCAN unit. Critical and acute High-acuity, time-critical care Critical Care Acute Care Emergency Orthopaedic Perioperative Child and family Newborns, children and their families Neonatal Paediatric Ageing and chronic care Long-term, complex and end-of-life care Gerontology Diabetes Palliative Care Renal Mental health Person-centred mental health practice Mental Health Popular starting points Full catalogue 01 / 12Critical and acute Critical Care Nursing Anticipate the change before it arrives. Registered nurses working in, or moving toward, ICU and high-acuity environments. Based on unit N8004Explore 07 / 12Ageing and chronic care Gerontology Nursing Dignity, expertise and leadership for later life. Registered nurses in aged care, rehabilitation and community settings caring for older people. Based on unit N8016Explore 03 / 12Critical and acute Emergency Nursing Clear thinking at the front door of the hospital. Registered nurses in emergency departments, urgent care and regional EDs. Based on unit N8008Explore Micro-credentials are proposed non-award units, not AQF qualifications. They do not confer registration, endorsement or a specialist title. This prototype does not collect partner enquiries; please contact IHM directly.',
